D("pressure group",{etymology:E("A compound of pressure + group; pressure here means organized influence, and the phrase names an organized body that tries to affect public policy or government decisions.",null,"pressure + group",null,"Oxford Learner’s Dictionaries, pressure group (https://www.oxfordlearnersdictionaries.com/definition/english/pressure-group)."),contexts:[C("pressure group.s1","政治","A pressure group organized a petition urging lawmakers to protect the river from industrial waste.","一個遊說團體發起請願，敦促立法者保護河流免受工業廢棄物污染。","A pressure group is an organized group that tries to influence government or public decisions.","Pressure group is the subject; urging lawmakers gives the group’s aim.","The organization campaigns to change a public policy decision.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The pressure group had no campaign or policy goal and existed only as a group that measured the air in tires.","The pressure group campaigned to influence the government’s environmental policy.","A pressure group seeks to influence decisions; a tire-measuring team is not one.")],quiz:[Q("pressure group.q1","pressure group.s1","meaning","A pressure group usually tries to ___.",["influence public or government decisions","repair household plumbing for a fee","measure weather without sharing results"],"influence public or government decisions","Its members apply organized pressure to decision-makers.","meaning"),Q("pressure group.q2","pressure group.s1","context","Which organization is a pressure group?",["A campaign coalition urging a law to protect wetlands","A shop selling gardening gloves","A private club with no public campaign"],"A campaign coalition urging a law to protect wetlands","The coalition is trying to influence a government decision.","context"),Q("pressure group.q3","pressure group.s1","word-choice","The ____ lobbied parliament to improve workplace safety.",["pressure group","prescription","presenter"],"pressure group","The organized body is lobbying for a public policy change.","word-choice"),Q("pressure group.q4","pressure group.s1","meaning","What distinguishes a pressure group from a casual crowd?",["It organizes to influence decisions on an issue.","It always gathers to watch a sports match.","It consists only of elected judges."],"It organizes to influence decisions on an issue.","The group’s purpose is organized influence or advocacy.","meaning")]});
D("pressurize",{etymology:E("Formed from pressure + -ize, the verb-forming suffix meaning ‘make or subject to’; pressurize means raise or maintain pressure, and figuratively to apply forceful persuasion.",null,"pressure","-ize","Etymonline, pressurize (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pressurize)."),contexts:[C("pressurize.s1","科學","Technicians pressurize the test chamber gradually so the seals can be checked at each level.","技術人員逐步提高測試艙內的壓力，以便在每個階段檢查密封狀況。","To pressurize is to raise or maintain the gas pressure in a container.","Pressurize takes the test chamber as its object; so introduces the purpose.","The chamber’s internal pressure is deliberately increased in stages."),C("pressurize.s1","社會","The recruiter tried to pressurize Ana into signing before she had time to read the contract.","招募人員試圖施壓，想讓安娜在還沒時間閱讀合約前就簽字。","Figuratively, pressurize means to urge someone forcefully to act.","Pressurize is followed by the person and into + action.","The recruiter is trying to force a decision before Ana can review the terms.")],mistakes:[M("collocation","The salesperson pressurized her for sign the contract before she read it.","The salesperson pressurized her into signing the contract before she read it.","The verb uses pressurize someone into doing something, with a gerund after into.")],quiz:[Q("pressurize.q1","pressurize.s1","meaning","To pressurize a container is to ___.",["raise or maintain the pressure inside it","remove every wall from it","paint it with a bright color"],"raise or maintain the pressure inside it","The physical verb concerns the gas pressure in a sealed space.","meaning"),Q("pressurize.q2","pressurize.s1","context","A manager pressurizes a worker into staying late when the manager ___.",["uses strong pressure to make the worker stay","offers the worker a free day off","asks once and accepts the answer"],"uses strong pressure to make the worker stay","The figurative sense is forceful urging.","context"),Q("pressurize.q3","pressurize.s1","collocation","Complete the pattern: “They pressurized him ___ accepting the offer.”",["into","at","by"],"into","The usual construction is pressurize someone into doing something.","collocation"),Q("pressurize.q4","pressurize.s1","word-choice","Before the test, engineers will ____ the sealed cabin to a safe level.",["pressurize","preside","pretend"],"pressurize","They raise the air pressure inside the cabin.","word-choice")]});
D("prestige",{etymology:E("From French prestige, from Latin praestigiae ‘illusions, conjuring tricks’; the word shifted from deception or glamour to the respect and influence associated with high status.",null,"praestigiae",null,"Etymonline, prestige (https://www.etymonline.com/word/prestige)."),contexts:[C("prestige.s1","社會","The small college gained prestige after its graduates led several important medical studies.","這所小型學院的畢業生主持了幾項重要醫學研究後，聲望隨之提升。","Prestige is respect or admiration associated with high status or achievement.","Prestige is the object of gained; after gives the reason for the change.","The graduates’ accomplishments cause others to respect the college."),C("prestige.s2","政治","The diplomat relied on the prestige of her office to persuade both sides to attend the talks.","外交官倚仗職位帶來的影響力，說服雙方出席會談。","Prestige can also be influence that comes from reputation or status.","Prestige is the object of relied on; to persuade states how it was used.","The office’s reputation gives the diplomat persuasive influence.")],mistakes:[M("countability","The school earned a prestige after its students won the national science prize.","The school earned prestige after its students won the national science prize.","Prestige in the sense of reputation is normally uncountable and is not used with a here.")],quiz:[Q("prestige.q1","prestige.s1","meaning","A university with prestige is ___.",["widely respected or admired","unknown to everyone and ignored","made only of glass"],"widely respected or admired","Prestige is respect associated with achievement or status.","meaning"),Q("prestige.q2","prestige.s2","context","A leader uses the prestige of an office to persuade others by relying on ___.",["the influence attached to that position","the office’s furniture","a private weather forecast"],"the influence attached to that position","The second sense is influence that comes from reputation or rank.","context"),Q("prestige.q3","prestige.s1","countability","Choose the standard sentence.",["The award gave the college prestige.","The award gave the college a prestige.","The award gave the college prestiges."],"The award gave the college prestige.","Prestige meaning respect or standing is generally uncountable.","countability"),Q("prestige.q4","prestige.s1","word-choice","Winning the international prize brought great ____ to the research institute.",["prestige","pressure","prescription"],"prestige","The award increases the institute’s reputation and standing.","word-choice")]});
D("prestigious",{etymology:E("Formed from prestige + -ous, an adjective suffix meaning ‘having or characterized by’; prestigious describes something that brings or enjoys respect.",null,"prestige","-ous","Etymonline, prestigious (https://www.etymonline.com/word/prestigious)."),contexts:[C("prestigious.s1","教育","Winning a place at the prestigious academy was difficult because thousands of students applied.","由於有數千名學生申請，進入這所享有盛譽的學院十分不易。","Prestigious means respected and admired for quality, success, or status.","Prestigious modifies academy; because explains its selectivity.","The academy is admired and attracts many applicants."),C("prestigious.s2","職場","She accepted a prestigious post at the museum, one that carried public honor and responsibility.","她接受了博物館一個顯赫的職位，該職位帶有公眾榮譽與責任。","A prestigious position can confer honor on the person who holds it.","Prestigious modifies post; that clause explains what the role carries.","The role itself is an honored appointment.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The award was prestigious because nobody respected it and every institution rejected its standards.","The award was prestigious because respected institutions valued its high standards.","Prestigious means admired or respected; universal rejection contradicts that meaning.")],quiz:[Q("prestigious.q1","prestigious.s1","meaning","A prestigious award is one that is ___.",["highly respected","given secretly to everyone","made of inexpensive paper"],"highly respected","Prestigious describes something admired for quality or status.","meaning"),Q("prestigious.q2","prestigious.s2","context","Which job is most likely to be called a prestigious post?",["A widely honored position leading a national museum","An unrecognized temporary task with no authority","An unpaid chore nobody values"],"A widely honored position leading a national museum","The position carries public honor and standing.","context"),Q("prestigious.q3","prestigious.s1","word-choice","The scientist received a ____ fellowship known for its rigorous selection.",["prestigious","pressing","presumable"],"prestigious","The fellowship is respected and highly selective.","word-choice"),Q("prestigious.q4","prestigious.s1","meaning","Why might a prestigious school attract many applicants?",["Its reputation makes people respect and value it.","It has no teachers or classes.","It forbids students from learning."],"Its reputation makes people respect and value it.","A prestigious institution is admired for its quality or standing.","meaning")]});
D("presumable",{etymology:E("Formed from presume + -able, meaning ‘able to be presumed’; presume comes from Latin praesumere ‘take beforehand’, from prae ‘before’ + sumere ‘take’.","prae-","sumere","-able","Etymonline, presume (https://www.etymonline.com/word/presume)."),contexts:[C("presumable.s1","書面","The presumable cause of the outage was a damaged cable, although engineers were still testing the equipment.","停電可以推測的原因是電纜受損，不過工程師仍在檢測設備。","Presumable means reasonably able to be inferred from what is known, though not yet proved.","Presumable modifies cause; although marks that the conclusion remains unconfirmed.","The available clues support the explanation, but testing is not complete.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The presumable cause was conclusively proved by direct evidence before investigators began examining the site.","The presumable cause was a reasonable inference that investigators still needed to test.","Presumable indicates something that can be inferred but is not yet established with certainty.")],quiz:[Q("presumable.q1","presumable.s1","meaning","A presumable explanation is ___.",["able to be reasonably inferred but not certain","proved beyond any possible doubt","impossible to imagine"],"able to be reasonably inferred but not certain","Presumable describes a plausible inference based on known facts.","meaning"),Q("presumable.q2","presumable.s1","context","Which is a presumable cause of a dark room when the power is out?",["A likely electrical failure, pending inspection","A confirmed solar eclipse that ended yesterday","A fact unrelated to the power system"],"A likely electrical failure, pending inspection","It is a reasonable explanation but awaits confirmation.","context"),Q("presumable.q3","presumable.s1","word-choice","Because the records are missing, a ____ reason for the delay is a filing error.",["presumable","presidential","pretended"],"presumable","A filing error is a plausible inference, though not yet proved.","word-choice"),Q("presumable.q4","presumable.s1","meaning","How does presumable differ from certain?",["It marks an inference that may still need confirmation.","It guarantees that no evidence exists.","It always refers to the future."],"It marks an inference that may still need confirmation.","The word leaves room for uncertainty.","meaning")]});
D("presumably",{etymology:E("From presumable + -ly, or the related adjective presume/presumptive family; it is an adverb used for a likely inference or what one reasonably expects.","prae-","sumere","-ably","Etymonline, presumably (https://www.etymonline.com/word/presumably)."),contexts:[C("presumably.s1","通用","The road is presumably open again, but the driver checked the official traffic notice before leaving.","道路大概已重新開放，但駕駛出發前先查看了官方交通公告。","Presumably signals that something is likely but not certain.","Presumably modifies the claim that the road is open; but adds a check against uncertainty.","The speaker makes a plausible inference while acknowledging it needs confirmation."),C("presumably.s2","通用","The package was presumably delivered yesterday, since the tracking page shows a completed drop-off.","包裹想必昨天已送達，因為追蹤頁面顯示投遞完成。","Presumably can express what one reasonably expects from the circumstances.","Presumably modifies was delivered; since introduces the evidence behind that expectation.","The tracking record makes delivery a reasonable expectation.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","She presumably knew the answer with absolute certainty, even though she had never seen the question or its subject.","She presumably knew the answer, although the evidence for that conclusion was limited.","Presumably marks a likely inference or expectation, not guaranteed certainty without evidence.")],quiz:[Q("presumably.q1","presumably.s1","meaning","The word presumably usually tells the reader that a statement is ___.",["likely but not certain","certainly false","a direct command"],"likely but not certain","The adverb marks an inference rather than complete proof.","meaning"),Q("presumably.q2","presumably.s2","context","“The shop is presumably open; its lights are on and customers are entering” expresses ___.",["a reasonable expectation based on clues","a proven fact with no possible exception","a wish that the shop would close"],"a reasonable expectation based on clues","The visible clues make the conclusion reasonable.","context"),Q("presumably.q3","presumably.s1","word-choice","The train is ____ delayed because the signal board lists a fault.",["presumably","preposition","prestigious"],"presumably","The fault gives a plausible reason to infer a delay.","word-choice"),Q("presumably.q4","presumably.s2","meaning","Which statement best captures the tone of “They presumably paid the bill”?",["One reasonably expects that they paid it.","The bill is known to be imaginary.","They are ordered to pay in the future."],"One reasonably expects that they paid it.","This use means as one would reasonably expect.","meaning")]});
D("presume",{etymology:E("From Old French presumer and Latin praesumere ‘take beforehand, assume’, from prae ‘before’ + sumere ‘take’; it developed senses of inference, legal assumption, and taking liberties.","prae-","sumere","-e","Etymonline, presume (https://www.etymonline.com/word/presume)."),contexts:[C("presume.s1","通用","I presume the bus is late because the street is still empty and its scheduled time has passed.","我推想公車晚了，因為街上仍空無一人，而且已過了預定時間。","To presume is to believe something likely without having proof.","Presume introduces a that-clause with the inferred claim; because gives the evidence.","The speaker draws a tentative conclusion from the timetable and empty street."),C("presume.s2","法律","The law presumes a defendant innocent unless the prosecution proves otherwise.","除非檢方證明相反，法律推定被告無罪。","In law, presume means accept a fact as true unless evidence shows otherwise.","Presumes takes the defendant innocent as its complement; unless sets the condition.","The legal starting point is innocence, subject to contrary proof."),C("presume.s3","社交","I would not presume to rearrange your files without asking you first.","我不會冒昧在沒先詢問你的情況下擅自整理你的檔案。","To presume can mean to act as if one has a right that one does not have.","Presume is followed by to + verb and the speaker denies taking that liberty.","The speaker recognizes that the files belong to someone else and seeks permission.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The guest presumed to enter the locked office without permission and later claimed that asking first would have been more presumptuous.","The guest did not presume to enter the locked office without permission and asked the owner first.","To presume to do something can mean to take an unwarranted liberty; asking permission avoids that act.")],quiz:[Q("presume.q1","presume.s1","meaning","If you presume the store is open from its usual schedule, you ___.",["infer that it is likely open without direct confirmation","know with absolute proof that it is open","order the owner to open it"],"infer that it is likely open without direct confirmation","Presume can mean believe something likely based on circumstances.","meaning"),Q("presume.q2","presume.s2","context","In law, a person is presumed innocent until ___.",["evidence proves guilt","the trial begins","a newspaper prints a headline"],"evidence proves guilt","The legal presumption stands unless contrary evidence is established.","context"),Q("presume.q3","presume.s3","grammar","Which sentence uses presume to mean “take an unwarranted liberty”?",["I would not presume to speak for the entire group.","I presume the meeting starts at noon.","The court presumes innocence."],"I would not presume to speak for the entire group.","Presume to speak means act as though one has the right to do so.","grammar"),Q("presume.q4","presume.s1","word-choice","Since the lights are off and the doors are locked, I ____ the museum has closed.",["presume","press","preserve"],"presume","The speaker makes a likely inference from the available clues.","word-choice")]});
D("presumption",{etymology:E("From Old French presumption and Latin praesumptionem, from praesumere ‘take beforehand, assume’; the word names an assumption or, figuratively, bold overconfidence.","prae-","sumere","-tion","Etymonline, presumption (https://www.etymonline.com/word/presumption)."),contexts:[C("presumption.s1","法律","The presumption of innocence remains in force unless the evidence establishes guilt.","除非證據證明有罪，無罪推定仍然有效。","A presumption is an assumption accepted unless it is disproved.","Presumption is the head of the phrase of innocence; unless sets the condition.","The legal assumption remains until contrary evidence meets the burden.") ,C("presumption.s2","社交","It was presumption to invite the director’s family without first asking whether they were welcome.","未先詢問是否受歡迎就邀請主管的家人，這樣做未免冒昧。","Presumption can mean rude overconfidence or acting beyond one’s right.","Presumption is the complement after was; to invite names the presumptuous act.","The invitation assumes permission that the speaker does not have.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The presumption of innocence means a court must treat every defendant as guilty before hearing any evidence.","The presumption of innocence means a court treats a defendant as innocent unless guilt is proved.","This legal presumption is an assumption of innocence, not guilt.")],quiz:[Q("presumption.q1","presumption.s1","meaning","A presumption is often ___.",["an assumption accepted unless disproved","a measurement made with a ruler","a confirmed photograph"],"an assumption accepted unless disproved","A presumption is treated as true until evidence overturns it.","meaning"),Q("presumption.q2","presumption.s2","context","Which action might be called presumption?",["Using a colleague’s office without permission because you assume it is yours","Asking whether you may borrow a chair","Waiting for an invitation before attending"],"Using a colleague’s office without permission because you assume it is yours","This behavior acts as though permission exists when it does not.","context"),Q("presumption.q3","presumption.s1","word-choice","The court applied the ____ that a person is innocent until proven guilty.",["presumption","presenter","pressure"],"presumption","The legal starting assumption is the presumption of innocence.","word-choice"),Q("presumption.q4","presumption.s2","meaning","In “She had the presumption to interrupt the judge,” presumption suggests ___.",["rude overconfidence","careful humility","legal proof of innocence"],"rude overconfidence","This sense criticizes bold behavior beyond one’s proper place.","meaning")]});
D("presumptuous",{etymology:E("From Medieval Latin praesumptuosus, from praesumere ‘take upon oneself, assume’; -ous forms an adjective, and the word describes acting with unwarranted confidence or entitlement.","prae-","sumere","-ous","Etymonline, presumptuous (https://www.etymonline.com/word/presumptuous)."),contexts:[C("presumptuous.s1","社交","It seemed presumptuous of the new intern to assign work to senior staff before learning the team’s roles.","新實習生還沒了解團隊分工，就指派資深員工工作，這看來有些冒昧。","Presumptuous describes acting as if one has more authority or importance than one does.","Presumptuous follows seemed; of the intern identifies who acted that way.","The intern assumes authority that has not been given.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","Her presumptuous request was modest and respectful because she asked politely for permission before using the room.","Her respectful request was modest because she asked permission before using the room.","Presumptuous means overconfident or taking liberties, not politely seeking permission.")],quiz:[Q("presumptuous.q1","presumptuous.s1","meaning","A presumptuous person may ___.",["act as if entitled to authority they do not have","wait politely for permission","avoid making any assumptions"],"act as if entitled to authority they do not have","The adjective criticizes unwarranted confidence or entitlement.","meaning"),Q("presumptuous.q2","presumptuous.s1","context","Which comment sounds presumptuous?",["“I will decide for the whole committee; no one needs to ask them.”","“May I offer a suggestion?”","“I should learn the rules before deciding.”"],"“I will decide for the whole committee; no one needs to ask them.”","The speaker assumes authority over others without permission.","context"),Q("presumptuous.q3","presumptuous.s1","word-choice","The visitor made a ____ demand to enter the private office without an invitation.",["presumptuous","pressing","presidential"],"presumptuous","The visitor acts as if entitled to enter without permission.","word-choice"),Q("presumptuous.q4","presumptuous.s1","meaning","Why can a presumptuous remark offend listeners?",["It assumes a right or status the speaker may not have.","It uses a quiet voice.","It describes a fact with careful evidence."],"It assumes a right or status the speaker may not have.","The word suggests overstepping one’s proper authority.","meaning")]});
D("presuppose",{etymology:E("Formed from pre- ‘before’ + suppose, from Latin supponere ‘put under’; to presuppose is to take something as already true or necessary before making an argument.","pre-","supponere","-ose","Etymonline, presuppose (https://www.etymonline.com/word/presuppose)."),contexts:[C("presuppose.s1","學術","The argument presupposes that every household has reliable internet access, an assumption the survey never tested.","這項論證預設每個家庭都有穩定網路，但調查從未檢驗這個假設。","To presuppose is to treat something as already true or necessary.","Presupposes introduces a that-clause stating the assumed condition.","The argument relies on a premise that has not been established.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The report presupposed that every resident had a car, then claimed it had proved this assumption without collecting any evidence.","The report stated an assumption that every resident had a car, but it did not prove that assumption.","Presuppose means take as a premise, not demonstrate that the premise is true.")],quiz:[Q("presuppose.q1","presuppose.s1","meaning","An argument that presupposes a fact ___.",["takes it as already true or necessary","proves it by definition","removes it from every discussion"],"takes it as already true or necessary","To presuppose is to rely on an assumed condition.","meaning"),Q("presuppose.q2","presuppose.s1","context","A study presupposes all participants can read English when ___.",["it assumes this without checking each participant’s language ability","it tests and records each participant’s reading ability","it translates every instruction into several languages"],"it assumes this without checking each participant’s language ability","The study treats the condition as given in advance.","context"),Q("presuppose.q3","presuppose.s1","word-choice","The conclusion would ____ that the missing records are accurate.",["presuppose","preserve","preside"],"presuppose","The conclusion relies on treating the records as accurate beforehand.","word-choice"),Q("presuppose.q4","presuppose.s1","meaning","Does presuppose itself mean “prove”?",["No; it means take something as an assumption.","Yes; it means prove beyond doubt.","Yes; it means erase evidence."],"No; it means take something as an assumption.","Presupposing a premise does not establish that it is true.","meaning")]});
D("pretence",{etymology:E("From Old French pretence and Latin praetendere ‘stretch forth, allege’, from prae ‘before’ + tendere ‘stretch’; the noun developed the sense of a claim or behavior meant to conceal the truth.","prae-","tendere","-ence","Etymonline, pretence (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pretence)."),contexts:[C("pretence.s1","社交","He maintained a pretence of confidence during the interview, though he had barely slept the night before.","他在面試時維持自信的假象，儘管前一晚幾乎沒睡。","A pretence is behavior that hides the truth or creates a false appearance.","Pretence is the object of maintained; of confidence specifies the appearance.","His outward confidence conceals his tiredness and anxiety.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","She dropped the pretence of being calm and continued smiling while privately feeling no emotion at all.","She dropped the pretence of being calm and admitted that she was frightened.","Dropping a pretence means stopping a false appearance, not continuing to perform it.")],quiz:[Q("pretence.q1","pretence.s1","meaning","A pretence is ___.",["a false appearance or act that hides the truth","a verified measurement","a permanent legal office"],"a false appearance or act that hides the truth","Pretence describes behavior used to conceal what is really happening.","meaning"),Q("pretence.q2","pretence.s1","context","Which person is putting on a pretence?",["Someone acts cheerful to hide disappointment.","Someone openly says they are disappointed.","Someone checks the time on a clock."],"Someone acts cheerful to hide disappointment.","The outward mood masks the true feeling.","context"),Q("pretence.q3","pretence.s1","word-choice","The argument was only a ____; he had already decided not to attend.",["pretence","pressure","prescription"],"pretence","The stated reason hides his real decision.","word-choice"),Q("pretence.q4","pretence.s1","meaning","What happens when someone drops a pretence?",["They stop maintaining the false appearance.","They make the false story more convincing.","They receive a formal appointment."],"They stop maintaining the false appearance.","To drop a pretence is to stop pretending.","meaning")]});
D("pretend",{etymology:E("From Old French pretendre and Latin praetendere ‘stretch forth, claim’, from prae ‘before’ + tendere ‘stretch’; the verb developed senses of falsely claiming, acting as if, and make-believe play.","prae-","tendere","-end","Etymonline, pretend (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pretend)."),contexts:[C("pretend.s1","通用","He pretended to understand the instructions, but his repeated questions showed that he was confused.","他假裝理解了指示，但一再提問顯示他其實很困惑。","To pretend can mean behave as if something untrue were true.","Pretended is followed by to + verb; but contrasts the act with evidence.","He gives a false impression of understanding."),C("pretend.s2","生活","The children pretended they were astronauts exploring a moon made of cardboard.","孩子們假裝自己是太空人，在紙板做的月球上探險。","To pretend can mean imagine a role in make-believe play without intending to deceive.","Pretended introduces a that-clause expressing the imagined role.","The children are playing imaginatively rather than trying to fool anyone."),C("pretend.s3","口語","He used a pretend passport made from folded paper during the game.","他在遊戲中使用了一本用摺紙做的假護照。","Pretend can be an adjective meaning not real, as used in play.","Pretend modifies passport and describes an imaginary prop.","The paper item is a toy document rather than an official passport.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The children pretended to be pirates only to deceive the police, so their make-believe game was a real criminal attack.","The children pretended to be pirates as part of an imaginative game, without claiming to be real pirates.","Pretend can describe playful make-believe; the sentence does not by itself mean the children committed an actual crime.")],quiz:[Q("pretend.q1","pretend.s1","meaning","If an adult pretends to be asleep, the adult ___.",["acts as though asleep while not actually being asleep","falls asleep without realizing it","teaches someone how to sleep"],"acts as though asleep while not actually being asleep","This sense creates a false appearance.","meaning"),Q("pretend.q2","pretend.s2","context","Which example is make-believe rather than deception?",["Children pretend a sofa is a spaceship during play.","A worker pretends to have a qualification on a job application.","A witness lies under oath."],"Children pretend a sofa is a spaceship during play.","The children are inventing an imaginary role for a game.","context"),Q("pretend.q3","pretend.s3","word-choice","The toddler wore a ____ crown made from gold paper during the game.",["pretend","presidential","presumable"],"pretend","The crown is a toy used in imaginative play, not a real crown.","word-choice"),Q("pretend.q4","pretend.s1","grammar","Which pattern correctly completes “She pretended ___ the message”?",["to have read","for read","reading to"],"to have read","Pretend can be followed by a to-infinitive; the perfect form indicates an earlier action.","grammar")]});
D("pretender",{etymology:E("From pretend + -er, an agent noun; the base goes back through Old French to Latin praetendere ‘claim or stretch forth’, and the noun names a claimant or someone falsely presenting an identity or skill.","prae-","tendere","-er","Etymonline, pretender (https://www.etymonline.com/word/pretender)."),contexts:[C("pretender.s1","歷史","The pretender claimed the empty throne, but the council refused to recognize his title.","這名王位覬覦者聲稱自己有權繼承空出的王位，但議會拒絕承認他的頭銜。","A pretender claims a position or identity that is disputed or not genuinely theirs.","Pretender is the subject; claimed states the claim and but contrasts the response.","The council’s refusal signals that his right to the throne is contested.")],mistakes:[M("meaning","The rightful heir was called a pretender because every court and legal document confirmed his claim without dispute.","The claimant was called a pretender because his right to the throne was disputed.","A pretender is someone whose claim to a position or identity is disputed or false, not an uncontested rightful holder.")],quiz:[Q("pretender.q1","pretender.s1","meaning","A pretender to a throne is someone who ___.",["claims the throne, often without an accepted right","officially records the king’s speeches","repairs the royal palace"],"claims the throne, often without an accepted right","The word names a claimant to a position such as a crown.","meaning"),Q("pretender.q2","pretender.s1","context","Which person could be called a pretender?",["Someone falsely claims to be a licensed doctor.","A doctor shows a valid license from the medical board.","A patient waits for an appointment."],"Someone falsely claims to be a licensed doctor.","The person falsely presents an identity or qualification.","context"),Q("pretender.q3","pretender.s1","word-choice","Several ____ disputed the royal succession after the monarch died.",["pretenders","presenters","preservatives"],"pretenders","They are rival claimants to the vacant position.","word-choice"),Q("pretender.q4","pretender.s1","meaning","What is often uncertain about a pretender?",["Whether the person truly has the right or ability they claim","Whether the person has ever eaten lunch","Whether the person can read a clock"],"Whether the person truly has the right or ability they claim","The term implies a disputed or false claim.","meaning")]});
